class:: SemiMarkov
summary:: Finite-state process with state-specific dwell distributions
categories:: UGens>Control>Markov
related:: Guides/Stochastica, Classes/MarkovChain, Classes/RenewalTrig

description::

A Semi-Markov model drastically expands the capability of a standard Markov chain. While it still uses a probability matrix to determine the next state, it also draws from continuous probability distributions to determine how long it remains in the current state before transitioning. Conceptually, this decouples structural sequencing from a rigid clock. It is incredibly powerful for full generative composition, allowing both harmonic shifts and rhythmic durations to be statistically guided and completely autonomous.

Combines a Markov transition matrix with a dwell-time distribution for each state. The duration Buffer has three channels: mean seconds, shape, and code (0 fixed, 1 exponential, 2 gamma, 3 Weibull, 4 lognormal).

At most one transition occurs per calculation and negative timing remainder is carried. Like link::Classes/MarkovChain::, it selects state from row-major weights; unlike MarkovChain, time rather than an external trigger schedules transitions.

subsection:: State and dwell view

image::semi-markov-dynamics.png#Left: a three-state semi-Markov realization using fixed, gamma, and Weibull dwell laws. Right: remaining dwell time counting down and resampling at transitions; the varying ramp heights make timing part of the model state.::

classmethods::

method:: ar
argument:: numStates
Init-rate state count, clipped to 1 through 256.
argument:: transitionBuf
One-channel row-major code::numStates*numStates:: weights.
argument:: durationBuf
Three-channel Buffer with code::numStates:: frames: mean, shape, distribution code.
argument:: valueBuf
Optional one-channel values; negative uses state index.
argument:: initialState
Rounded and clipped initial state.
argument:: timeScale
Nonnegative countdown speed. Zero pauses.
argument:: seed
Init-rate local seed.
argument:: reset
Restore state and seed and schedule its first duration.
returns:: State, mapped value, one-calculation changed trigger, remaining seconds.

method:: kr
Control-rate version with the same arguments and outputs.

instancemethods::

examples::

code::
(
Routine {
    b = Buffer.loadCollection(s, [0.8, 0.2, 0.3, 0.7]);
    c = Buffer.loadCollection(s, [1, 1, 0, 0.2, 1, 1], 3);
    s.sync;
    x = { SemiMarkov.kr(2, b, c)[0].poll }.play;
}.play;
)
::

code::
(
Routine {
    b = Buffer.loadCollection(s, [
        0.7, 0.2, 0.1,
        0.2, 0.6, 0.2,
        0.1, 0.3, 0.6
    ]);
    c = Buffer.loadCollection(s, [
        0.6, 1, 0,
        0.2, 2, 2,
        1.4, 0.5, 3
    ], 3);
    d = Buffer.loadCollection(s, [180, 420, 900]);
    s.sync;
    x = {
        var process = SemiMarkov.kr(3, b, c, d);
        SinOsc.ar(process[1], 0, Decay2.kr(process[2], 0.01, 0.2) * 0.1)
    }.play;
}.play;
)
::

code::
// Decoupled Structural Sequencing
(
Routine {
    var trans, durs, vals;
    
    // 3 states: 
    // 0: Rapid, percussive plucks (short durations)
    // 1: Mid-length bouncy FM chords
    // 2: Long, sweeping atmospheric pads
    trans = Buffer.loadCollection(s, [
        0.1, 0.6, 0.3, // State 0 mostly goes to 1
        0.4, 0.2, 0.4, // State 1 goes to 0 or 2
        0.8, 0.1, 0.1  // State 2 almost always goes back to 0
    ]);
    
    // Duration buffer: 3 frames, 3 channels (mean, shape, dist_code)
    // Codes: 0=fixed, 2=gamma, 4=lognormal
    durs = Buffer.loadCollection(s, [
        0.1, 1.0, 0, // State 0: exactly 0.1s
        0.4, 2.0, 2, // State 1: ~0.4s, gamma distributed
        2.5, 0.5, 4  // State 2: ~2.5s, lognormal distributed
    ], 3);
    
    vals = Buffer.loadCollection(s, [60, 67, 55]); 
    s.sync;
    
    {
        var process, state, freq, trig, sig0, sig1, sig2;
        
        // numStates, transitionBuf, durationBuf, valueBuf
        process = SemiMarkov.kr(3, trans, durs, vals);
        state = process[0];
        freq = process[1].midicps;
        trig = process[2]; // triggers exactly when state changes
        
        // Sound design changes drastically based on the current state
        sig0 = Pluck.ar(WhiteNoise.ar, trig, 0.2, freq.reciprocal, 0.5, 0.1);
        sig1 = PMOsc.ar(freq, freq * 1.5, EnvGen.kr(Env.perc(0.01, 0.3), trig) * 3);
        sig2 = LPF.ar(Saw.ar([freq, freq * 1.01]), 800) * EnvGen.kr(Env.perc(0.1, 2.0), trig);
        
        // Select avoids control-rate boolean multiplication errors and is highly idiomatic
        Select.ar(state, [
            Pan2.ar(sig0, 0),
            Pan2.ar(sig1, 0),
            sig2 * 0.2
        ])
    }.play;
}.play;
)
::
